# Command substitutions ($(...)) used to be evaluated with no job-parent information at
# all (EvalContext carried no job handle), so a substitution's own job always showed up
# in jobs as an unrelated top-level job with no parent -- the same class of bug
# exit's "are there other jobs running" check had for closures/blocks before it
# was taught to recognize an ancestor job, except command substitutions went unfixed at
# the time. Fixed by threading a parent job id through EvalContext (via
# From<&JobContext>/From<&CommandContext>) into JobContext::new_nested whenever a
# substitution is evaluated as part of some other job's own execution.
#
# This used to check a *backgrounded* substitution's parent (`$(sleep ... &)`, captured
# from a later, sibling statement's own jobs snapshot) -- but that's exactly the
# false-parentage shape tests/job_orphaning_on_parent_death.crush now protects against:
# the statement that spawns a background job returns (and is reaped) essentially
# instantly, well before any later statement can even run, so a later snapshot could
# only ever see a *stale* parent id there, never a live one -- and once dying jobs
# correctly orphan their children (clearing `parent` instead of leaving that stale id
# behind for a subsequent job to coincidentally recycle), there's no id left for a later
# statement to match against at all. Confirmed empirically: on unfixed code this test
# was actually passing only because the reaped statement's freed id got handed straight
# back to the very next job (the snapshot-taking one itself), making the stale parent
# field match by sheer numeric coincidence, not genuine ancestry.
#
# The property this test actually needs -- a substitution genuinely nested inside
# another job that's still running -- has to be observed while the parent job provably
# hasn't returned yet, not via any job created strictly *after* it returns. Argument
# evaluation gives exactly that: `list:of` can't start running until its own argument,
# the inner `$(jobs | materialize)` substitution, finishes evaluating -- so while
# that substitution is busy capturing its snapshot, the `list:of` job is unquestionably
# still alive (its own Job::eval is on the stack, blocked evaluating that very
# argument). No race, no reliance on reaping timing. (`list:of` specifically, rather
# than e.g. `echo`, because it passes its argument through unchanged with no stdout side
# effect, so the snapshot table itself -- not some derived/printed value -- is what ends
# up captured in $snapshot.)
$snapshot := $($(list:of $(jobs | materialize))[0])

# The snapshot-taking job itself should show up with a real parent job id recorded (not
# empty/none) -- matched by definition prefix, the same technique used elsewhere in this
# suite, since `like`/`=~` only do exact equality against a string pattern despite the
# name.
$snapshot_rows := $($snapshot | where {$definition:starts_with "\$jobs "} | materialize)
$snapshot_row_count := $($snapshot_rows | count)
assert ($snapshot_row_count == 1) "the jobs substitution itself should be visible in its own snapshot"
$snapshot_parent_id := $($($snapshot_rows | select parent | list:collect)[0])

# ...and that parent id should itself point at another job that's *also* visible in the
# very same snapshot -- the still-running `list:of` job that's blocked waiting on this
# very substitution to finish, not a dangling/empty reference. (If $snapshot_parent_id
# were Empty, comparing it against an Integer id column below would fail per-row and
# this filter would simply come back empty, correctly failing the count check -- no
# separate type check needed.)
$parent_rows := $($snapshot | where {($id == $snapshot_parent_id)} | materialize)
$parent_row_count := $($parent_rows | count)
assert ($parent_row_count == 1) "the substitution's recorded parent should itself be a visible, still-running job in the same snapshot"

# ...and that parent job's own definition should indeed be the enclosing `list:of`, not
# merely some unrelated job that happens to share its id.
$parent_definition := $($($parent_rows | select definition | list:collect)[0])
assert ($parent_definition:starts_with("\$list:of ")) "the recorded parent should be the enclosing list:of job, not an unrelated one"
